<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haitian Vodou</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vodou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haitian Vodou is an Afro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> religion that developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> among enslaved Africans and their descendants, combining West and Central African traditions with elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. It incorporates spirits and practices with roots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> and Fon religion. Its Yoruba elements are cited as part of the cultural legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> in the Atlantic world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou.jpg" alt="Haitian Vodou" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sam Fentress · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VoodooValris.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Vodou took shape amid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> of the 16th to 19th centuries, when the religions of enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, Fon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba people</a> brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> mixed with the Roman Catholicism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freemasonry">Freemasonry</a> of the French who held the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>; it had taken the form of Vodou by around the mid-18th century. Haitian folklore holds that a Vodou ritual at Bois-Caïman sparked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> in 1791, though historians debate such claims. After the Revolution the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> for several decades, allowing Vodou to become the country's dominant religion.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> occupation of 1915 to 1934 drew outside interest, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>'s middle-class indigenist movement came to view Vodou more favourably. In 1941 the government launched Operation Nettoyage, a Church-backed campaign that destroyed many ounfòs, while President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-duvalier">François Duvalier</a> later hailed Vodou as &quot;the supreme factor of Haitian unity&quot;. Emigration in the 20th century carried the religion abroad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Vodou teaches the existence of a transcendent creator, Bondye, from the French Bon Dieu, who is remote from human affairs. Below Bondye are the lwa, spirits who usually take their names from West and Central African deities, are equated with Catholic saints and fall into nanchon, or nations, chief among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> and the Petwo. Practitioners, who call themselves sèvitè, those who serve the lwa, seek the aid of the spirits and of the dead with offerings of food, liquor and sacrificed animals.</p><p>There is no central authority. Communal worship centres on the ounfò, or temple, run by an oungan or a manbo, a priest or priestess, and initiation typically has four levels. In the nocturnal ceremonies known as the dans, drumming, song and dance invite a lwa to possess a worshipper and speak through them. Drawings called vèvè, traced on the floor in cornmeal or ash, summon the lwa, and divination and herbal healing also play a large part.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Most Haitians practise both Vodou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> without seeing a contradiction, and several scholars see Haitian religion as a continuum between the two. In 1992 Desmangles put the number of Haitian practitioners at six million. Various scholars describe Vodou as one of the world's most maligned and misunderstood religions: Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> have often presented it as Satanic, and films such as White Zombie (1932) have sensationalised it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haitian Vodou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of them captives of Dahomean wars, left the Bight of Benin through Ouidah and neighbouring ports, and their religious traditions shaped the Americas: the vodun of the Fon and their neighbours is a principal root of <b>Haitian Vodou</b> and of the Tambor de Mina tradition of northern Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Their religious practices became foundational to Candomblé in Brazil and Santería (Lucumí) in Cuba, while Yoruba, or Nago, elements also entered <b>Haitian Vodou</b> alongside its predominantly Fon and Kongo roots.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Nearly all speak Haitian Creole, French the educated 42%; 55% were Catholic and 29% Protestant in 2018, while 50–80% keep some element of <b>Vodou</b>, recognised by the state in 2003.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>This produced a broad free peasantry, unusual in the plantation Americas, but it ended large-scale sugar exports, reduced state revenue and left a gulf between the rural majority, who spoke Haitian Creole and practised <b>Vodou</b> alongside Catholicism, and a French-speaking urban elite of officials, merchants and landowners.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vodou</b>, blending Catholic ritual with the religions of Guinea, Congo and Dahomey, was outlawed and driven underground, but healed and held them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It is the best known and most popular of the Afro-Cuban religions, which include Palo, Abakuá and Arará, and it shares its origins in Yoruba religion with <b>Haitian Vodou</b> and Brazilian Candomblé, sometimes called its sister religions.</blockquote><a href="/religion/santeria">Santería · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
